**Proof.**

Let $a=BC,\ b=CA,\ c=AB$ and $\alpha=\angle BAC,\ \beta=\angle ABC,\ \gamma=\angle ACB$. The hypothesis $AB>BC$ gives $c>a$.

Define $P$ to be the point on the ray from $B$ through $C$ with $BP=BA=c$; since $c>a=BC$, this point lies beyond $C$ on line $BC$. We show $I,J,P$ are collinear for every $D$.

Place coordinates $B=(0,0),\ C=(a,0),\ A=(c\cos\beta,\,c\sin\beta)$, so that $P=(c,0)$.

Write $x=\angle BAD\in(0,\alpha)$, so $\angle DAC=\alpha-x$, and by construction $\angle BAE=\alpha-x$, $\angle CAE=x$.

---

**The incenter $I$ of $\triangle ABD$.** In $\triangle ABD$ we have $\angle ABD=\beta$, $\angle BAD=x$, $\angle ADB=\pi-\beta-x$, hence by the sine rule
$$BD=\frac{c\sin x}{\sin(\beta+x)},\qquad AD=\frac{c\sin\beta}{\sin(\beta+x)}.$$
The incenter has barycentrics $(BD:AD:AB)$ w.r.t. $(A,B,D)$, so (using $B=(0,0)$ and $D=(BD,0)$)
$$I=\frac{BD\cdot A+AD\cdot B+c\cdot D}{BD+AD+c}.$$
With $S:=\sin x+\sin\beta+\sin(\beta+x)$ we have $BD+AD+c=\dfrac{cS}{\sin(\beta+x)}$, giving
$$I=\frac1S\bigl(c\sin x(1+\cos\beta),\; c\sin\beta\sin x\bigr). \tag{1}$$

**The incenter $J$ of $\triangle ACE$.** Since $E$ is on the circumcircle on the side of $BC$ opposite $A$, the inscribed angle $\angle AEC=\angle ABC=\beta$; together with $\angle CAE=x$ this yields $\angle ACE=\pi-\beta-x$ and $\angle BCE=\alpha-x$. The sine rule in $\triangle ACE$ gives
$$CE=\frac{b\sin x}{\sin\beta},\qquad AE=\frac{b\sin(\beta+x)}{\sin\beta}.$$
The point $E$ lies below $BC$ on the ray from $C$ making angle $\alpha-x$ with $CB$, so
$$E=\Bigl(a-\tfrac{b\sin x}{\sin\beta}\cos(\alpha-x),\;-\tfrac{b\sin x}{\sin\beta}\sin(\alpha-x)\Bigr).$$
From $J=\dfrac{CE\cdot A+AE\cdot C+b\cdot E}{CE+AE+b}$ and $CE+AE+b=\dfrac{bS}{\sin\beta}$ we obtain
$$J=\frac1S\Bigl(c\sin x\cos\beta+a(\sin(\beta+x)+\sin\beta)-b\sin x\cos(\alpha-x),\;\sin x\bigl(c\sin\beta-b\sin(\alpha-x)\bigr)\Bigr). \tag{2}$$

---

**Collinearity with $P$.** The line $IJ$ meets the $x$-axis ($BC$) at the point whose $x$-coordinate equals
$$\frac{I_yJ_x-I_xJ_y}{I_y-J_y}.$$

From (1) and (2),
$$I_y-J_y=\frac{b\sin x\,\sin(\alpha-x)}{S}.$$

Writing $I=\frac1S(I_x',I_y')$, $J=\frac1S(J_x',J_y')$ for the $S$-free numerators, a direct expansion gives
$$I_y'J_x'-I_x'J_y'=\sin\gamma\,\sin\beta\,\sin x\cdot Q,$$
where (using $a:b:c=\sin\alpha:\sin\beta:\sin\gamma$, i.e. $2R=1$)
$$Q=-\sin\gamma\sin x-\sin\beta\sin x\cos(\alpha-x)+(1+\cos\beta)\sin x\sin(\alpha-x)+\sin\alpha\sin(\beta+x)+\sin\alpha\sin\beta.$$
Therefore the intersection of $IJ$ with $BC$ has $x$-coordinate
$$\frac{\sin\gamma\,\sin\beta\,\sin x\,Q/S^{2}}{b\sin x\sin(\alpha-x)/S}=\frac{\sin\gamma\,Q}{S\sin(\alpha-x)}.$$

It remains to verify that $Q=S\sin(\alpha-x)$. Expanding both sides with $\cos(\alpha-x)=\cos\alpha\cos x+\sin\alpha\sin x$, $\sin(\alpha-x)=\sin\alpha\cos x-\cos\alpha\sin x$, $\sin(\beta+x)=\sin\beta\cos x+\cos\beta\sin x$ and $\sin\gamma=\sin(\alpha+\beta)$, one collects terms by the monomials $1,\sin x,\cos x,\sin x\cos x,\sin^{2}x,\cos^{2}x$:

$$Q=\bigl[\sin\alpha+\sin(\alpha-\beta)\bigr]\sin x\cos x-\bigl[\cos\alpha+\cos(\alpha-\beta)\bigr]\sin^{2}x-\cos\alpha\sin\beta\sin x+\sin\alpha\sin\beta\cos x+\sin\alpha\sin\beta,$$

while

$$S\sin(\alpha-x)=\bigl[\sin\alpha+\sin(\alpha-\beta)\bigr]\sin x\cos x-\cos\alpha(1+\cos\beta)\sin^{2}x-\cos\alpha\sin\beta\sin x+\sin\alpha\sin\beta\cos x+\sin\alpha\sin\beta\cos^{2}x.$$

The coefficients of $\sin x\cos x$, $\sin x$, $\cos x$ agree. The remaining difference is
$$Q-S\sin(\alpha-x)=-\bigl[\cos(\alpha-\beta)-\cos\alpha\cos\beta\bigr]\sin^{2}x-\sin\alpha\sin\beta\cos^{2}x+\sin\alpha\sin\beta.$$
Since $\cos(\alpha-\beta)-\cos\alpha\cos\beta=\sin\alpha\sin\beta$, this becomes
$$-\sin\alpha\sin\beta\,\sin^{2}x-\sin\alpha\sin\beta\,\cos^{2}x+\sin\alpha\sin\beta=0.$$
Hence $Q=S\sin(\alpha-x)$, and the intersection of $IJ$ with $BC$ is the point
$$\frac{\sin\gamma\cdot S\sin(\alpha-x)}{S\sin(\alpha-x)}=\sin\gamma=c,$$
i.e. exactly $P=(c,0)$.

Thus for every position of $D$ the line $IJ$ passes through $P$, the point on the ray $BC$ beyond $C$ satisfying $BP=BA$. This point depends only on the triangle $ABC$, not on $D$. $\quad\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
